akes here on  c5 and then after queen two a5  putting pressure both from the queen and  the knight here on  c3 we see the move e4  and i think this is an interesting spot  for a lots of people because  you have this square here on c3 it's not  defended at all  you have two pieces that can attack this  you have the immediate check here with  queen takes here on c3 so  who cares about my knight being attacked  but you can also just take with your  knight here on c3 it's defended by  the queen here this is attacking the  queen here on  uh d1 as soon as it moves it's a  discovered attack but actually  this would be a bad move knight takes  here on  c3 then you see queen to d2 now the  knights pin down to the queen  it can take it as soon as the bishop  comes here to b2 on the next move so you  could see  knight to c6 here uh bishop to  b2 now the knight can move and only  because  it's it was pinned but the knight here  on c6 is defending the queen  but now after the queen takes the knight  takes here bishop takes here on  g7 the rook moves bishop back here to  f6 and you can see both the knights here  on the a file  not exactly where they want to be you  have  whites with a much better setup here  up in material here  all in all not a spot that wesley so  wants to be and even if he takes with  his queen doesn't have to take with his  knight for it to be a mistake it would  still be bad if he takes with his queen  here on  c3 because bishop to d2 the queen is  forced to move here  if he takes on c5 that's even worse  because you you may look and say okay  where does the queen move here but queen  to c5  loses to rook to c1 and there's the  queen pretty much has to fall you could  come back here to  uh c7 which doesn't but you could just  take here on c1  and just give up the queen but if you  move something like d6 as an example you  can see already  rook takes here on c8 and then do you  want to give up your queen now or  do you just want to move your king and  just give up more material either way  it's going to lose you could also  try uh queen 2 e5 that's another way to  escape here  but then white's just going to continue  to attack and so you could play  knight 2 e2 setting up bishop to  c3 here  the knight's under attack from the pawn  on e4 so it comes back to e7 black's  definitely in  a retreat mode bishop to c3 but i can't  get anything going  on while white's just continuing to push  up the board here  and this is a lot of pressure that  wesley snow so does not want  to be under so in this position right  here west is so correctly  which i think a lot of people would  incorrectly play but he plays knight  to e7 now bishop to  e3 castle on the king side queen to  b3 then the queen comes back here to c7  now usually what happens in the nimzo  indian  is that white is up material  and you can see right here that white  has seven pawns to the six pawns of  black but there is the  uh double isolated pawns here on  uh the c file it's gonna be very  difficult for black to hold or for white  to hold on to both of these and so  eventually  black is usually able to capture that  material and then black should have  a good in game  now we see bishop 2 b5 just get involved  into the action  wants to get the night out so you can  castle on the king side  if he wants to doesn't have to castle  for sure  but knight to e2 here bishop to e6 just  moving the pawn up to e5 so the bishop  could come to the center of the board  and attack the queen  instead of moving the queen decides you  could just push up forward here with  c4 knight to uh  c6 knight here to c3  knight to a5 it could always take here  but it doesn't really do too much  because then the knight's just gonna  take here it's exactly the position that  black has but all of a sudden white  doesn't have the bishop hair  that is the one thing that white has  going for him  and so sam needs to take advantage of  that at some point he needs to open up  the board really use  that 