eally  calculated he's eyeing up here  this d5 square which makes a lot of  sense  bishop takes here on d5 pawn takes here  on d5  what i don't think he realized is that  black was no longer going to  attack this square here on d5 instead  wesley so is going to play  queen to a3  attacking the square here on f3  and you can see that's not going to go  well at all that's going to end in a  very quick  checkmate so sam really has to  protect the pawn the only way to do that  is to play d6 that's exactly what he  does  but then queen two b2 and now the threat  is the queen coming over here to  g2 and there's no way to really stop  that so  yeah you can bring your queen down here  to e4 that's fine but queen g2  king over here e1 you're going to lose  your rook here on h1  check king 2 d2 you can see stuff  starting to fall apart here gobbling up  material  queen takes here on h2 then all of a  sudden you have wesley so one of the  best players in the world with  an active queen a knight here on an  outpost that can start to get involved  into the action a rook here that  any time could take the bishop on a8 but  it can also swing into the action  start to attack the king here things are  not going to go well with an exposed  king like this knight to e2 getting  involved check  all of a sudden knight 2 d4 still a  great outpost here for the knight  uh d7 sure pushing up but you're never  gonna have a chance to do anything with  the promotion there  queen two a two and then a couple moves  later we do  have the end of the game and the  simulations were running  and it was showing that this was gonna  be mate  and not too many moves uh you know eight  or nine moves to depending on  uh where white ends up going so at the  end uh sam  does decide to resign wesley so wins not  only did he win  round four ended up winning round five  as well now did draw  game number six it almost seemed like he  was going to go back-to-back days  winning every single game but he sits  atop  